The code statements that lead to the bug are:
xfs_ail_push_all_sync(mp->m_ail);
xfs_inodegc_stop(mp);
cancel_delayed_work_sync(&mp->m_reclaim_work);
Reason:
The AIL is pushed while inodegc and background reclaim are still active. This allows inodegc to dirty and insert new inodes into the AIL after the push, and background reclaim can race to abort and free dirty inodes, leading to use-after-free and inconsistencies.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
xfs_inodegc_stop(mp);
cancel_delayed_work_sync(&mp->m_reclaim_work);
xfs_ail_push_all_sync(mp->m_ail);
Reason:
Stopping inodegc first prevents it from dirtying and inserting inodes into the AIL during the flush; canceling background reclaim prevents racing with inode freeing. Only after both are stopped is the AIL pushed, ensuring a clean state for subsequent reclaim.

